# Managed by homelab-postfix -- do not hand-edit, edit debconf answers
# instead (dpkg-reconfigure homelab-postfix). Replaces the vendor's own
# /etc/opendkim.conf wholesale (OpenDKIM has no drop-in include
# directory the way Postfix/PowerDNS do) -- only written at all when
# the homelab-postfix/enable_dkim debconf flag is true.
#
# KeyTable/SigningTable are regenerated by homelab-domain-admin itself
# (see ../../domain-admin/README.md's "DKIM" section) every time a
# selector is rotated/activated/retired -- this package only creates
# them empty (with the right ownership) and points OpenDKIM at them.
# Only 'active' selectors ever appear in these two files: OpenDKIM only
# consults them when SIGNING outbound mail, never when a remote server
# verifies a signature (that's a pure DNS TXT lookup) -- so a 'pending'
# or 'retiring' selector correctly has no KeyTable/SigningTable entry
# at all, even though its key file and TXT record both still exist.
# "Safe keys" (post-cutover #68, homelab-postfix 0.1.29 with
# homelab-domain-admin >= 0.1.45): every private key under
# /etc/opendkim/keys is opendkim:opendkim mode 0600, put there by root --
# homelab-domain-admin writes its own copy under /var/lib/homelab/opendkim
# and hands it over with `sudo homelab-opendkim-install` -- so OpenDKIM's
# own check passes and stays ON. Before 0.1.29 the keys were shared through
# group opendkim with the homelab service user as a second member; the
# check could never pass, it was disabled here, and OpenDKIM warned "key
# data is not secure: root is in group ... which has multiple users" once
# per signature (benign, but on every signed message). The postinst writes
# `no` here only while an older homelab-domain-admin is still installed.
#
# UserID: the daemon must RUN as opendkim for those keys to count as safe
# -- the check compares the key directory's owner with the executing uid,
# and until 0.1.30 this template had no UserID line at all, so OpenDKIM
# ran as root (the Debian unit sets no User=) and refused the handed-over
# keys with "/etc/opendkim/keys is writeable and owned by uid N which is
# not the executing uid (0)" (found live on prod-homelab39, 2026-11-11).
# Debian's own default config has this line; it is also simply the right
# privilege for a mail filter. /run/opendkim (PidFile) is owned by opendkim
# via the package's tmpfiles entry; the inet socket is unprivileged.
RequireSafeKeys yes
UserID          opendkim
Syslog          yes
UMask           007
Mode            sv
Domain          *
KeyTable        /etc/opendkim/KeyTable
SigningTable    /etc/opendkim/SigningTable
ExternalIgnoreList  refile:/etc/opendkim/TrustedHosts
InternalHosts       refile:/etc/opendkim/TrustedHosts
Socket          inet:8891@__MILTER_BIND_ADDRESS__
PidFile         /run/opendkim/opendkim.pid
